.TH units "" "Command"
.SH NAME
units \- measurement conversion
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBunits [\-u]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
units -- measurement conversion
Usage:	units [ -u ]
Option:	-u	Update binary file only
Units works interactively.
.HE
.B units
is an interactive program that computes the conversion factor between
two units of the same dimension.
It prints the prompt
.B "You have:"
and reads the quantity you wish to convert from, then prints
.B "You want:"
and reads the quantity you wish to convert to.
If the two are conformable (the same dimension), it prints the
multiplicative factor to convert the first quantity into the second,
and its reciprocal; otherwise it prints
.B Conformability
followed by each quantity broken down into its fundamental units, and
prompts again.
.B units
loops asking for pairs of quantities until it reaches end of file on
the standard input.
.PP
A quantity is a number (default 1, if omitted) followed by a unit
expression (default dimensionless, if omitted).
A unit expression is built from named units, optionally scaled by a
metric prefix (such as
.B kilo
or
.BR milli ),
raised to a power with a trailing
.B "+n"
or
.BR "\-n" ,
and combined with
.B /
for division and adjacent units for multiplication; the words
.B per
(divide),
.B square
(power 2), and
.B cubic
(power 3) may also be used.
.IP \fB\-u\fR
Read the ASCII units table and rewrite the binary units file, then
exit without prompting for any conversion.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 16n
\fB/usr/lib/units\fR	table of known units, in ASCII
\fB/usr/lib/binunits\fR	the same table, precompiled to binary
.DE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B units
prints
.B "Rebuilding /usr/lib/binunits from /usr/lib/units ..."
and regenerates the binary table whenever
.B /usr/lib/units
is newer than
.B /usr/lib/binunits
or the latter is missing or unreadable; this can take a while.
It prints
.B "Unrecognised unit"
followed by the offending name for a word it cannot match against any
known unit, and
.B Conformability
for a pair of quantities that do not have the same dimension.
.SH NOTES
.B units
is installed set-user-id to
.B bin
so that it can write the shared binary table
.B /usr/lib/binunits
the first time it is regenerated; it drops this privilege (by calling
.BR setuid ())
before doing any conversion.
